Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Ext4 implementation verification, 2026-11-15

Implementation: fs/ext4.zero, original Zero source, no imported guest driver or
assembler/linker. The unchanged state-machine fragment is concatenated with
ext4/host-prefix.zero and host-suffix.zero, then compiled by bootstrap/zero
--host. The adapter maps the kernel's state90 request ABI onto own compiler
pread/pwrite/fsync primitives and deliberately clobbers all volatile scratch
variables after every I/O. FD3 is a disposable filesystem image. Optional FD4
configures volume offset/length and injected I/O failure; it is not guest code.

Reproduce: bash ext4/run-tests.sh
Fixture/check tools: e2fsprogs 1.47.2, separately unpacked Debian binaries at
/workspace/ext4-tools/root. Set ZERO_EXT4_E2FSPROGS to another tool root, or
ZERO_EXT4_COMPILER to another verified own Zero compiler if required.

Passed cases:
* Minimal standard ext4 extents + filetype, 4 KiB blocks, 256-byte inodes.
* Filetype + direct block maps, 4 KiB blocks, 128-byte inodes.
* Empty creation, read, save-create, overwrite, truncate empty, delete, recreate.
* 400-byte text and 31-byte names; 401 bytes/32-byte names refused.
* Existing files written by debugfs read and updated by Zero; Zero-created
  contents independently read by debugfs.
* Multiple inode-table blocks and reuse after deleting their former contents.
* Independent e2fsck -fn exit0 after successful mutation batches.
* Real exhaustion of free inodes, free data blocks, and supported directory
  capacity; refusal leaves the entire image byte-identical.
* Invalid names, wrong magic, journal/checksum defaults, dirty filesystem,
  malformed directory record length, inconsistent free counters, short I/O.
* Every read/write applies a nonzero partition start at LBA2048; the extracted
  standard partition is accepted by e2fsck.
* Twelve separate write/flush failure points return I/O status3; failures after
  the unclean marker and before the clean-marker write cause a fresh probe to
  return recovery-required status9. Final flush failure is reported, never
  claimed as durable success.
* A cross-inode data alias with otherwise matching allocation bitmaps/counters
  refuses overwrite and deletion before mutation, for direct and extent maps.
* A referenced block forged free with matching counters cannot be newly
  allocated; unsupported indirect ownership maps refuse mutation.

The final host suite passes 268 assertions. Independent source review by the
compiler agent covered cache aliasing, scratch-value lifetime, bounds, and
flush ordering. Its separate corrupt-alias fixture now returns corruption2 on
overwrite/delete and retains the same whole-image SHA256 after both attempts.

The tests found and fixed a real inode-reuse defect: clearing a deleted inode's
extent header while retaining EXTENTS made later checking of that table block
fail. Deleted inodes now clear that obsolete flag, and reuse passes e2fsck.
The host adapter also now accumulates reads until EOF: a pipe read can return
only part of a request. A deliberately fragmented pipe tests that correction.
Six consecutive fresh-fixture runs passed after the adapter correction.

This report primarily covers the exact host-executed driver logic. The main
project also recorded a BIOS64 VM integration run at
verification/fs-0.7.0/bios64/early.serial: B:| ext4 save/read/remove/persist and
same-name isolation across ZeroFS/ext4/NTFS. Its ext4-check.txt records a clean
independent e2fsck pass. Other BIOS/UEFI configurations and final release media
are covered by the main project tests, not inferred from the host checks. The profile
limits in ext4-profile.txt remain intentional: this is not full general ext4,
journal replay, metadata checksums, 64-bit groups, hierarchical traversal,
binary/large files, directory growth or power-failure atomicity.
